    "math_academy_id": "q-48720",
    "knowledge_point_id": "20b289d4-96a7-5d1b-9714-6afabab4804d",
    "knowledge_point": "Further Determining Rates of Change in Geometrical Settings",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13948186/assets/2b5d589462bf2477be3d305ea88f3d5462b7d6ea4e001a9a71790d53e1683665.png)\n\nThe area of a square is increasing. Find the instantaneous rate at which the area of a square is increasing with respect to the length of its side at the moment when the length of the sides is equal to $l=6\\,\\text{cm}.$",
    "worked_solution": "We know that the area of the square with respect to $l$ is given by  \n\n$$\nA=A(l)={l}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $A(l)$  with respect to $l$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(l) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}l}({l}^{2})=2l,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $l=6$ gives\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(6)=2(6)=12.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate at which the area of the square is increasing when the length of the side is $l=6\\,\\text{cm}$ is $12\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{cm}.$",
